In this lesson we'll look at the three laws of logarithms, and the great news is they all come straight from the index laws you've already met. Here's the first idea, the product law: whenever you multiply two numbers inside a single log, you can just add their separate logs together. So two logs collapse into one sum. Next, the quotient law: dividing inside the log turns into subtracting one log from the other. And finally, the power law, which is probably my favourite. If something is raised to a power inside the log, that power simply slides down and multiplies the log in front. Why does all this work? Because logarithms are really just exponents in disguise. A log of x in base a is really asking, "to what power do I raise a to get x?" So whenever you add exponents, you're multiplying the original numbers, and whenever you subtract exponents, you're dividing them. That direct link is what makes all three laws feel so natural. Before we wrap up, a couple of handy consequences to keep in mind: the log of one is always zero, the log of a matching base is one, and a base raised to its own log just gives you the argument back. Now let's see it in action.